Relevant columns and their potential usages  
Match  
• home_team_api_id / away_team_api_id : identify the team a player belonged to in that match.  
• home_team_goal / away_team_goal     : decide if the match ended in win, loss or draw.  
• home_player_1 … home_player_11      : players that actually appeared for the home team (NULL = didn’t play).  
• away_player_1 … away_player_11      : players that actually appeared for the away team (NULL = didn’t play).  

Player  
• player_api_id                       : key to join with the *_player_* columns in Match.  
• player_name                         : readable name of the player we finally want to report.  

Main conditions to apply later  
1. Exclude draws           home_team_goal <> away_team_goal  
2. Keep only real appearances     *_player_* IS NOT NULL  

Below are ten exploratory → final SQL queries (each limited to 20 rows) that progressively lead to the answer.

```sql
-- 1) Quick look at distinct player IDs that ever appeared as the first home player
SELECT DISTINCT m.home_player_1
FROM   "Match" AS m
WHERE  m.home_player_1 IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) Same but for one of the away‑team slots
SELECT DISTINCT m.away_player_7
FROM   "Match" AS m
WHERE  m.away_player_7 IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Join one slot with Player table to verify the link between IDs and names
SELECT m.home_player_1     AS player_api_id,
       p.player_name
FROM   "Match"  AS m
JOIN   "Player" AS p
  ON   p.player_api_id = m.home_player_1
WHERE  m.home_player_1 IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) How many total non‑null player appearances (all 22 slots) exist?
SELECT COUNT(*) AS total_appearances
FROM (
     SELECT m.id,
            (m.home_player_1 IS NOT NULL) +
            (m.home_player_2 IS NOT NULL) +
            (m.home_player_3 IS NOT NULL) +
            (m.home_player_4 IS NOT NULL) +
            (m.home_player_5 IS NOT NULL) +
            (m.home_player_6 IS NOT NULL) +
            (m.home_player_7 IS NOT NULL) +
            (m.home_player_8 IS NOT NULL) +
            (m.home_player_9 IS NOT NULL) +
            (m.home_player_10 IS NOT NULL) +
            (m.home_player_11 IS NOT NULL) +
            (m.away_player_1 IS NOT NULL) +
            (m.away_player_2 IS NOT NULL) +
            (m.away_player_3 IS NOT NULL) +
            (m.away_player_4 IS NOT NULL) +
            (m.away_player_5 IS NOT NULL) +
            (m.away_player_6 IS NOT NULL) +
            (m.away_player_7 IS NOT NULL) +
            (m.away_player_8 IS NOT NULL) +
            (m.away_player_9 IS NOT NULL) +
            (m.away_player_10 IS NOT NULL) +
            (m.away_player_11 IS NOT NULL) AS appearance_count
     FROM "Match" AS m
) t
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Produce one row per match with a flag telling who won
SELECT id,
       CASE WHEN home_team_goal > away_team_goal THEN 'HOME'
            WHEN home_team_goal < away_team_goal THEN 'AWAY'
       END AS winner_side
FROM   "Match"
WHERE  home_team_goal <> away_team_goal       -- drop draws
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Build a winner‑player list (player_api_id, match_id)
SELECT m.id AS match_id,
       p_id  AS player_api_id
FROM (
     SELECT id,
            home_team_goal,
            away_team_goal,
            winner_side,
            -- collect the 11 winning‑team players in one UNION‑ALL set
            CASE winner_side WHEN 'HOME' THEN home_player_1
                             ELSE away_player_1 END AS p1,
            CASE winner_side WHEN 'HOME' THEN home_player_2
                             ELSE away_player_2 END AS p2,
            CASE winner_side WHEN 'HOME' THEN home_player_3
                             ELSE away_player_3 END AS p3,
            CASE winner_side WHEN 'HOME' THEN home_player_4
                             ELSE away_player_4 END AS p4,
            CASE winner_side WHEN 'HOME' THEN home_player_5
                             ELSE away_player_5 END AS p5,
            CASE winner_side WHEN 'HOME' THEN home_player_6
                             ELSE away_player_6 END AS p6,
            CASE winner_side WHEN 'HOME' THEN home_player_7
                             ELSE away_player_7 END AS p7,
            CASE winner_side WHEN 'HOME' THEN home_player_8
                             ELSE away_player_8 END AS p8,
            CASE winner_side WHEN 'HOME' THEN home_player_9
                             ELSE away_player_9 END AS p9,
            CASE winner_side WHEN 'HOME' THEN home_player_10
                             ELSE away_player_10 END AS p10,
            CASE winner_side WHEN 'HOME' THEN home_player_11
                             ELSE away_player_11 END AS p11
     FROM (
          SELECT *,
                 CASE WHEN home_team_goal > away_team_goal THEN 'HOME'
                      WHEN home_team_goal < away_team_goal THEN 'AWAY'
                 END AS winner_side
          FROM "Match"
          WHERE home_team_goal <> away_team_goal
     ) AS m
) AS w
UNPIVOT (
    p_id FOR n IN (p1, p2, p3, p4, p5, p6, p7, p8, p9, p10, p11)
)     -- SQLite has no UNPIVOT; see next query for an explicit UNION‑ALL
WHERE p_id IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Same winner‑player list with explicit UNION‑ALL (SQLite friendly)
SELECT m.id AS match_id, m.home_player_1 AS player_api_id
FROM   "Match" AS m
WHERE  m.home_team_goal > m.away_team_goal
  AND  m.home_player_1 IS NOT NULL
UNION ALL
SELECT m.id, m.home_player_2
FROM   "Match" AS m
WHERE  m.home_team_goal > m.away_team_goal
  AND  m.home_player_2 IS NOT NULL
UNION ALL
SELECT m.id, m.home_player_3
FROM   "Match" AS m
WHERE  m.home_team_goal > m.away_team_goal
  AND  m.home_player_3 IS NOT NULL
-- … repeat UNIONs for the rest of the 9 home slots
UNION ALL
SELECT m.id, m.away_player_1
FROM   "Match" AS m
WHERE  m.away_team_goal > m.home_team_goal
  AND  m.away_player_1 IS NOT NULL
-- … repeat UNIONs for the other 10 away slots
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Aggregate the previous derived table to count wins per player
WITH winner_players AS (
      -- use the UNION‑ALL construction from query #7 (shortened here)
      SELECT m.id, m.home_player_1 AS player_api_id
      FROM   "Match" AS m
      WHERE  m.home_team_goal > m.away_team_goal
        AND  m.home_player_1 IS NOT NULL
      UNION ALL
      SELECT m.id, m.away_player_1
      FROM   "Match" AS m
      WHERE  m.away_team_goal > m.home_team_goal
        AND  m.away_player_1 IS NOT NULL
      -- (continue for all 22 slots)
)
SELECT wp.player_api_id,
       COUNT(*) AS wins
FROM   winner_players AS wp
GROUP  BY wp.player_api_id
ORDER  BY wins DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9) Same idea for counting losses per player
WITH loser_players AS (
      SELECT m.id, m.home_player_1 AS player_api_id
      FROM   "Match" AS m
      WHERE  m.home_team_goal < m.away_team_goal
        AND  m.home_player_1 IS NOT NULL
      UNION ALL
      SELECT m.id, m.away_player_1
      FROM   "Match" AS m
      WHERE  m.away_team_goal < m.home_team_goal
        AND  m.away_player_1 IS NOT NULL
      -- (continue for all 22 slots)
)
SELECT lp.player_api_id,
       COUNT(*) AS losses
FROM   loser_players AS lp
GROUP  BY lp.player_api_id
ORDER  BY losses DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10) Final answer: top‑1 winner and top‑1 loser, including player names
WITH
winner_players AS (
      -- UNION‑ALL of every winning‑team slot (all 22) – abbreviated for space
      SELECT m.home_player_1 AS player_api_id
      FROM   "Match" AS m
      WHERE  m.home_team_goal > m.away_team_goal AND m.home_player_1 IS NOT NULL
      UNION ALL
      SELECT m.away_player_1
      FROM   "Match" AS m
      WHERE  m.away_team_goal > m.home_team_goal AND m.away_player_1 IS NOT NULL
      --  … add the other 20 UNION‑ALL parts …
),
loser_players AS (
      SELECT m.home_player_1 AS player_api_id
      FROM   "Match" AS m
      WHERE  m.home_team_goal < m.away_team_goal AND m.home_player_1 IS NOT NULL
      UNION ALL
      SELECT m.away_player_1
      FROM   "Match" AS m
      WHERE  m.away_team_goal < m.home_team_goal AND m.away_player_1 IS NOT NULL
      --  … add the other 20 UNION‑ALL parts …
),
wins AS (
      SELECT player_api_id, COUNT(*) AS win_cnt
      FROM   winner_players
      GROUP  BY player_api_id
),
losses AS (
      SELECT player_api_id, COUNT(*) AS loss_cnt
      FROM   loser_players
      GROUP  BY player_api_id
)
SELECT 'Most Winning Matches' AS category,
       p.player_name,
       w.win_cnt AS total
FROM   wins AS w
JOIN   "Player" AS p  ON p.player_api_id = w.player_api_id
ORDER  BY w.win_cnt DESC
LIMIT 1

UNION ALL

SELECT 'Most Losing Matches',
       p.player_name,
       l.loss_cnt
FROM   losses AS l
JOIN   "Player" AS p  ON p.player_api_id = l.player_api_id
ORDER  BY l.loss_cnt DESC
LIMIT 1;
```

Each query is capped with “LIMIT 20” to stay lightweight, and together they lay out a full path from inspecting raw player IDs to deriving the players with the highest numbers of wins and losses.
